 <!--
   CATAPULT LAUNCH
   ==========================================================================
   [Q, cover page] "Катапультний старт" - no landing gear [Q 7.2].

   SIZING (see MODEL-NOTES.md for the full working):
     VS at the typical 34.36 kg all-up = 23.2 m/s (VS1 = 24.4 m/s at MTOW 38 kg
     per Q 3.1/3.2). Target release = 34 m/s = 1.46 VS, so the wing is already
     flying - and, critically, flying at a LOW alpha - the instant it leaves
     the rail.
       F = m*dV/dt = 34.36 * 34 / 0.40 s = 2921 N = 657 lbf
       a = 8.7 g,  stroke = 0.5*a*t^2 = 6.8 m  (a realistic pneumatic rail)
     SIM_JSBSim.cpp sets the magnitude from SITL_CATAPULT_LBF (660 for this
     airframe; the Geran-2 default is 4900 for its 200 kg).

   DIRECTION: pure +X body, i.e. straight along the fuselage axis.
     The rail IC is 5 deg nose-up, so the aircraft leaves at gamma = +5 deg
     with alpha = 0 - and at alpha = 0 this wing still makes CL0 = 0.091
     because of its +3 deg wing setting angle [Q 2.4]. Contrast the Geran-2,
     whose force is tilted 15 deg up in BODY axes: that produces a velocity
     vector 15 deg ABOVE the body axis, i.e. alpha = -15 deg at release, and
     costs it a 3 m post-release sink. Keeping the thrust line along the body
     axis avoids that failure mode entirely.

   ATTACHMENT: on the CG (x=0.991, z=0).  A real rail carries the reaction
   through the belly, but it also restrains the airframe in pitch for the whole
   stroke - which JSBSim's forces/hold-down cannot do once it is released. An
   off-CG force here would instead integrate freely: 0.12 m of offset at 2921 N
   is 350 N*m against Iyy=7.8, i.e. 45 rad/s^2, which tumbles the aircraft
   inside the 0.4 s stroke. A CG-line force reproduces "the rail holds the
   attitude and only accelerates it forward", which is what the hardware does.
 -->
 <external_reactions>
  <force name="catapult" frame="BODY">
   <location unit="M">
    <x> 0.991 </x>
    <y> 0.000 </y>
    <z> 0.000 </z>
   </location>
   <direction>
    <x> 1.0 </x>
    <y> 0.0 </y>
    <z> 0.0 </z>
   </direction>
  </force>
 </external_reactions>

</fdm_config>
